### Problem

Originally we followed the approach of applying user overrides the same way base-contracts did [here](https://github.com/base/contracts/blob/6ca464d118d2d33331ea44893f0930f753663f60/script/universal/NestedMultisigBase.sol#L124-L133):

```
    function _overrides(address _signerSafe, address _safe) internal view returns (Simulation.StateOverride[] memory) {
        Simulation.StateOverride[] memory simOverrides = _simulationOverrides();
        Simulation.StateOverride[] memory overrides = new Simulation.StateOverride[](2 + simOverrides.length);
        overrides[0] = _safeOverrides(_signerSafe, MULTICALL3_ADDRESS);
        overrides[1] = _safeOverrides(_safe, address(0));
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < simOverrides.length; i++) {
            overrides[i + 2] = simOverrides[i];
        }
        return overrides;
    }
```

Notice how the `simOverrides` are appended to the end of the `overrides` array. This leads to undesirable behavior in Tenderly. I personally observed how ALL existing state overrides for a contract were completely overridden when a user specified even just a single override for the same contract. This behavior was confirmed by Tenderly: 

> Based on this, keys should always be unique and if two identical are provided only one will be taken into account, can not guarantee that it will always be the last one.

The reason base doesn’t hit this tenderly bug is because the use an environment variable “[SAFE_NONCE](https://github.com/base/contracts/blob/6ca464d118d2d33331ea44893f0930f753663f60/script/universal/MultisigBase.sol#L38)” to get the nonce for the simulation. It **ISN’T** added the to [_simulationOverrides](https://github.com/base/contracts/blob/6ca464d118d2d33331ea44893f0930f753663f60/script/universal/MultisigBase.sol#L212C14-L212C34) function. The “SAFE_NONCE” is added to the default overrides. They would hit this issue if a user tried to pass a state override for a parent or child save inside the `_simulationOverrides` function. 

We've been able to 'get away' with this for now because users aren't specifying complex state overrides. However, on the odd occasions we might've seen the Tenderly link break. 


### Solution
Get all state overrides for simulation. Combines default Tenderly overrides with user-defined overrides. User defined overrides either replace or append to the default overrides.


